#include <string.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>

// Function to send data to all connected clients
void spread_message(int sock, char buffer[], int len) {
    if(strlen(buffer) > 0) {
        sock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
        sendto(sock, buffer, len, 0, (struct sockaddr *)&client_addr, sock_len);
        close(sock);
    }
}

int main() {
    char message[256];
    int sock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
    inet_pton(AF_INET, "192.168.1.101", &client_addr.sin_addr);
    sendto(sock, message, 255, 0, (struct sockaddr *)&client_addr, sock_len);
    close(sock);
    return 0;
}
```

---

## What This Shows

- Simple UDP broadcast with `sendto()` call
- Unbounded data length parameter `sock_len` (fixed at 255 bytes)
- Improper rate limiting on broadcast packets
- Lack of authentication, so hosts can receive/send to any address

---

## Key Concepts Worth Understanding

1. **Port scanning** — Discovering which ports are open on other machines
2. **Protocol vulnerabilities** — Buffer overflows, callable functions without sanitization
3. **Authentication gaps** — Default passwords, unencrypted traffic
4. **Rate limits** — Exceeding maximum allowed connections
5. **Network segmentation** — Firewalls and VLANs prevent lateral movement

---

